{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ca9mruzKPu6B69gstn5oRA","type":"tool_result","content":"All-Agent Conference — Dean Proposal.md:38:[Omitted long matching line]\nAGENTS.md:100:- `taste_model`: `anthropic/claude-opus-4-7`\nAGENTS.md:101:- `reasoning_model`: `anthropic/claude-opus-4-7`\nAGENTS.md:103:- The `/use-opus-this-turn` slash command in `agent-chat-v0.mjs` remains for one-turn escalation but is now redundant given Opus is the default; consider deprecating in a future cleanup.\nADDENDUM-P11.5-Source-Aptness-and-Watcher-Memory.md:997:2. **Pre-call gate:** `question-quality-check` expert runs *after* Frontal makes a cloud-frontier call (expensive, wasteful). It should run *before* the call to gate vague/template-bound queries and bounce them back to Frontal for re-prompt construction.\nADDENDUM-P11.5-Source-Aptness-and-Watcher-Memory.md:1037:   Before dispatching to Frontal for cloud-frontier calls, inject a\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:5:**Relationship to v2.** v3 does not supersede v2. v2 specifies the **Taste Agent** — an excellent client of cloud frontier models. v3 specifies the **Reasoning Agent** — a Taste Agent with a parallel \"reasoning brain\" trained via online distillation from the frontier model's responses on the taste agent's curated curriculum. Both agent types coexist in the project; they are deliberately different things, with different cost / latency / hardware profiles.\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:11:v2 ended with the Taste Doctrine (§14): Alpha's competitive advantage is curation, framing, and discernment — being an excellent client of the frontier model. That doctrine is correct, but it has a ceiling. As long as every meaningful turn has to call the cloud frontier model, the agent pays frontier-tier latency (1–3s) and frontier-tier cost ($0.05–0.30) per turn. Frontier-leverage can grow, but it cannot grow *unboundedly* unless some of the reasoning happens locally.\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:13:[Omitted long matching line]\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:21:[Omitted long matching line]\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:29:| Frontal cell calls | cloud frontier (Gemini Pro / Claude Opus) | shadow-split: local fine-tuned LLM AND cloud frontier |\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:34:| What it learns from | episodic + curriculum + dream-loop perturbations | all of the above, PLUS frontier responses as gold labels |\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:61:            A_local                            A_frontier\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:78:1. **Always frontier.** Use A_frontier. A_local recorded only as training signal. Simple, slow, expensive. The Phase 2 default — gives a clean baseline so you can prove the mechanism is working before granting it routing authority.\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:79:2. **Confidence-gated.** Reasoning brain emits A_local with calibrated confidence. If confidence > threshold AND Watcher coherence-check passes, use A_local. Otherwise use A_frontier. The steady-state mode after the agent has internalized.\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:113:- **Librarian manifest** — local-LLM manifest-builder. Adapter `taste-librarian-vN` on Qwen-3-7B (Phi-4-14B on workstation). Gold signal: which manifest items ended up referenced in the Frontal output, weighted by frontier-leverage of the resulting response?\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:114:- **Frontal** — calls *cloud* frontier (Gemini Pro / Claude Opus). NOT fine-tuned (no local model here at all). The Frontal in the taste brain stays cloud because the whole point is that the cloud frontier *is* the gold answer.\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:120:Has its own **Frontal cell** with experts `plan-local`, `critique-local`, `integrate-local`. Provider is `local-reasoning`. Base model: Qwen-3-7B (workstation default) or Phi-4-14B (workstation with 48GB+ VRAM). LoRA adapter `alpha-reasoning-vN`. Gold signal: the frontier model's responses A_frontier on the same prompts P that the taste brain assembled (the (Q, A_frontier) distillation pairs of §4–§5).\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:134:   cloud-frontier   local-reasoning\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:139:     A_frontier   A_local\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:155:- The cells *upstream of Frontal* (manifest building, shard retrieval, sensory dispatch) are computing **what context to put in front of the model**. They are upstream of the question. There's no \"frontier vs local\" split there because there's no model output to compare yet.\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:156:- The cells *downstream of Frontal* (Watcher, Consolidator) are auditing and consolidating **the model's output and what to learn from it**. They benefit from seeing both A_frontier and A_local in one place — comparator logic is naturally a Watcher concern.\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:161:### 3.6 The frontier-vs-local prediction gate (added 2026-04-30 PM)\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:163:A new mechanism sits *between* the taste brain's prompt assembly and the Frontal split: the **frontier-vs-local prediction gate**. The gate predicts, before any model call, whether A_local is likely to be at least as good as A_frontier on this specific prompt. When it predicts yes with high confidence, the agent skips the cloud frontier call entirely and uses A_local directly.\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:165:The gate is the **8th Watcher expert**: `frontier-vs-local-gate`. It is a small classifier (logistic regression or 2-layer MLP) on top of:\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:178:[Omitted long matching line]\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:184:    skip frontier; use A_local for user\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:185:    with probability 0.05 (audit): also call frontier in shadow,\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:192:The audit probability is the safety latch. Even when the gate is confident, 5% of skipped-frontier turns still call the frontier in shadow mode. Those audit turns produce ground truth: was the gate right? If A_frontier turned out clearly better than A_local on an audit, that pair becomes a hard negative for the gate's next training cycle.\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:194:Training the gate: nightly batch on the (P, gate_decision, A_frontier, A_local, judged_quality) tuples produced by all shadow + audit turns. Positive example = A_local judged equivalent or better; negative = A_frontier meaningfully better. Loss: binary cross-entropy. Compute cost: trivial (small classifier on embeddings; minutes on CPU per night).\n\n[Showing results with pagination = limit: 30]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01R7onNqhV8BYzhXq32rMA1B","session_id":"75eda862-34e1-4a42-a5f6-73eb4ef1cd9a","uuid":"2366637e-a80c-4041-9d92-e68f8374dd63","timestamp":"2026-06-23T18:17:42.535Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Ground Gandalf diagnosis of Agentic-Home"}
