{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LuZo4JA473DJiAz6QBKAz5","type":"tool_result","content":"AGENTS.md:103:- The `/use-opus-this-turn` slash command in `agent-chat-v0.mjs` remains for one-turn escalation but is now redundant given Opus is the default; consider deprecating in a future cleanup.\nAGENTS.md:152:- Cognitive cells (v1+), QLoRA students (v2+) — out of v0 scope.\nADDENDUM-P11.5-Source-Aptness-and-Watcher-Memory.md:9:[Omitted long matching line]\nADDENDUM-P11.5-Source-Aptness-and-Watcher-Memory.md:66:  \"suspected_gap\": \"source-class-mismatch: planned for time-sensitive but question is contemplative\",\nADDENDUM-P11.5-Source-Aptness-and-Watcher-Memory.md:697:  Post-hoc audit: compare planned source usage vs actual source usage.\nADDENDUM-P11.5-Source-Aptness-and-Watcher-Memory.md:705:  1. Planned high-weight source not used (e.g., planned live-data but used\nADDENDUM-P11.5-Source-Aptness-and-Watcher-Memory.md:707:  2. Used source lower-ranked than planned. Verdict = revise (suggest\nADDENDUM-P11.5-Source-Aptness-and-Watcher-Memory.md:715:  If high confidence + all sources as planned, verdict = pass.\nADDENDUM-P11.5-Source-Aptness-and-Watcher-Memory.md:765:       \"mismatch_type\": \"planned-source-not-used\",\nACTION-PLAN-Hillsdale-and-Curriculum-2026-05-03.md:35:1. Re-encode the existing log to UTF-8 (overwrite in place). All future writes UTF-8.\nACTION-PLAN-Hillsdale-and-Curriculum-2026-05-03.md:434:  9. Genesis — anchors Western Civ I, English II, the future Theology /\nCODER-PROMPT-PACK.md:109:- `agent-chat.js` — at agent bootstrap, after SOUL parse: read every `regions.<r>.seed_llm`, call `getSeed(id)` for each, throw on missing/retired with a clear message naming the agent and region. If `~/gbrain/agents/<agentId>/training-fork.json` does NOT exist, write it atomically with `{seed_manifest_hash, seeds_per_region: {<region>: <seed_id>, ...}, instantiated_at: <ISO-timestamp>, lora_lineage: []}`. Future Tier-3 LoRA training events (G-12) append to `lora_lineage`.\nCRITIQUE-PASS-1-v5-proto.md:86:[Omitted long matching line]\nCRITIQUE-PASS-1-v5-proto.md:132:**Verdict: AMEND.** **Amend §5 to:** explicitly mark which brain-side workstreams are *self-sustaining* on existing corpus (Tier-3 distillation on current tuples; replay scheduler on existing index) vs which need *fresh* in-harness input (drift detection of new model variants; future training pairs from v0). The drafted \"parallel\" framing hides that one track feeds the other; failure in one starves both. Also: state explicitly what happens to brain-side work if v0 is delayed beyond S2.\nCLAUDE_hist.md:16:[Omitted long matching line]\nCLAUDE_hist.md:17:[Omitted long matching line]\nCLAUDE_hist.md:18:[Omitted long matching line]\nCLAUDE_hist.md:24:[Omitted long matching line]\nAll-Agent Virtual Conference — Dean Proposal.md:52:[Omitted long matching line]\nCLAUDE.md:7:[Omitted long matching line]\nCLAUDE.md:9:[Omitted long matching line]\nCLAUDE.md:11:[Omitted long matching line]\nCLAUDE.md:13:[Omitted long matching line]\nCLAUDE.md:75:Everything else in `routing.json` (`dean`, `lambda`, `phi`, `ccr`, `w-*`, `s-*`) is planned but not yet built. The `AgentMatrix.tsx` component grays them out and offers to spawn an Interactive Builder when clicked.\nCLAUDE.md:256:- `C:\\Users\\john\\gwl-corpus\\` — **user-profile training-data root** (formerly `~/gbrain/`, renamed 2026-05-15 per B-11). Outside the workspace, but the brain code reads/writes here. Don't relocate; brain-code path strings (10 production files, 6 test files) point at `path.join(os.homedir(), 'gwl-corpus', 'agents', ...)`. See `PATH-RENAME-design.md` for the rename history.\nCLAUDE.md:312:[Omitted long matching line]\nCRITIQUE-Alpha-v1.2-Context-Intelligence-2026-05-23.md:28:The user's framing — *\"the runtime override collapses the split\"* — is **mostly right, partially wrong**. The dispatcher at `t2r-dispatcher.mjs:660-669` already overrides only the reasoning model, not the taste. The structural risk was that the single `TIER_TO_MODEL` name invited future regression. **That risk is now closed.**\nCRITIQUE-Alpha-v1.2-Context-Intelligence-2026-05-23.md:93:The override at (c) flows only into the reasoning slot. The taste model is whatever `resolveModelPair` returned upfront from `routing.json`. **The structural split was already enforced at runtime** — what was wrong was the **naming** (`TIER_TO_MODEL` lied about its scope) and the **risk** that a future refactor could accidentally use the same table for the taste half.\nCRITIQUE-Alpha-v1.2-Context-Intelligence-2026-05-23.md:135:Four independent binary dimensions = **16 distinct context profiles**. The plan does not enumerate them. State-space explosion is a real cost: every distinct profile is a different cache prefix, a different LoRA training stratum, and a different combination an engineer must understand.\nCRITIQUE-Alpha-v1.2-Context-Intelligence-2026-05-23.md:149:The taste model still emits the nested object (for G-27 LoRA training); the dispatcher canonicalizes to one of five labels via a `PROFILE_TO_PAYLOAD` lookup before prompt assembly. Five labels are LoRA-trainable; sixteen are not.\nCRITIQUE-Alpha-v1.2-Context-Intelligence-2026-05-23.md:221:**Correct on the structural risk.** The single map invited future regressions. **Mostly wrong on the runtime impact**, because `t2r-dispatcher.mjs:660-669` only fed the override into the `reasoningModelOverride` slot — never `tasteModelOverride`. So today's runtime already preserves the split for fielded code. The risk was latent.\nCRITIQUE-Alpha-v1.2-Context-Intelligence-2026-05-23.md:256:| Research / brain-pilot | `alpha-linear`, `alpha-lorentz`, `sigma` | match reasoning model | Tokenizer consistency for LoRA training. |\nCRITIQUE-Alpha-v1.2-Context-Intelligence-2026-05-23.md:261:To make the split explicit per agent (and prevent future regressions), add to `routing.json` agent entries:\nCRITIQUE-Alpha-v1.2-Context-Intelligence-2026-05-23.md:329:| **R26 (new)** | `TIER_TO_MODEL` name invites future regression that tier-overrides taste | **RESOLVED 2026-05-23** — renamed to `REASONING_TIER_TO_MODEL`; alias preserved |\nCRITIQUE-Alpha-v1.2-Context-Intelligence-2026-05-23.md:330:| **R27 (new)** | 16-state `context_load` space breaks LoRA stratification + cache hit rate | OPEN — collapse to 5 profiles + `custom` |\nCRITIQUE-Alpha-v1.2-Context-Intelligence-2026-05-23.md:435:**Effect:** `signals.resource` and `envelope.halves.reasoning.model` now agree on every turn. Before: telemetry showed the pre-tier routing.json default; envelope showed the post-tier model actually used. The G-27 corpus that trains the LoRA gets the correct values; live signals downstream consumers (dashboard, training-pipeline-stats) get the correct values.\nCRITIQUE-Alpha-v1.2-Context-Intelligence-2026-05-23.md:466:- **Dispatcher integration to call `resolveReasoningModel` instead of using `TIER_TO_MODEL` directly.** `t2r-dispatcher.mjs:661` and `gwl-t2r-brain/src/index.mjs:222` currently use the alias. Functionally identical; renames will let the alias warning fire on future code that touches these lines. Safe to land at any time.\nBRAIN-ARCHITECTURE-REVIEW.md:13:3. **Split-Brain Economics:** Using cloud frontier models to generate training data for local, continuously fine-tuned models is a brilliant solution to the cost/latency bottleneck of modern agents.\nBRAIN-ARCHITECTURE-REVIEW.md:14:4. **Offline Neuroplasticity:** The concept of \"Sleep\" (Tiers 1-3) to perform garbage collection, gate-residual nudging, and LoRA adapter branching represents a leap forward in continuous agent learning.\nBRAIN-ARCHITECTURE-REVIEW.md:22:While theoretically beautiful, the architecture flirts with over-engineering. The hot path requires multiple model invocations per turn, risking latency compounding. Furthermore, automated structural neuroplasticity (Tier 3 LoRA tuning) carries a massive risk of catastrophic forgetting if the Pareto loss evaluation fails to detect subtle regressions. The integration of Poincaré (hyperbolic) embeddings is cutting-edge but mathematically volatile in continuous training setups.\nBRAIN-ARCHITECTURE-REVIEW.md:36:[Omitted long matching line]\nBRAIN-ARCHITECTURE-REVIEW.md:56:- **Hyperbolic Embeddings (Poincaré Memory):** GWL's `Knowledge-Poincare` implementation is highly forward-looking. Standard LLM RAG uses Euclidean vector spaces, which struggle with hierarchical data. Recent papers (e.g., *HypLoRA*, 2024) confirm that hyperbolic space exponentially fits tree-like knowledge graphs better. However, the literature also notes that mapping back and forth between Euclidean LLM spaces and Hyperbolic memory can cause representation collapse if not handled carefully.\nBRAIN-ARCHITECTURE-REVIEW.md:62:- **Avoidance of Prompt Bloat:** Instead of stuffing the context window with rules, GWL structurally alters the model (gate residuals, LoRA) to adapt. \nBRAIN-ARCHITECTURE-REVIEW.md:67:- **Complexity Collapse:** The system relies on dozens of moving parts interacting perfectly. The strict barrier acks (G-11), the Habit Graveyard queries, and the MoE gate residuals all must stay in sync. Debugging a logic failure in a split-brain agent with a mutated LoRA and a decayed strategy shard will be nearly impossible, even with the Brain Surgeon CLI.\nBRAIN-ARCHITECTURE-REVIEW.md:79:   For Tier 3 deep sleep, mandate that all perturbation searches occur in an isolated \"shadow\" environment. Before any LoRA weights or MoE gates are permanently updated, the proposed changes must undergo a micro-GLCI benchmark (a random sampling of the K-12 unit exams) to prevent catastrophic forgetting.\nBRAIN-ARCHITECTURE-REVIEW.md:83:   Update the instructions for G-12 to explicitly require the creation of a local `.tar.gz` snapshot of the `cell.config.json` and LoRA weights immediately prior to the perturbation search loop, allowing the `git revert` command to cleanly restore binary model state alongside code state.\nAll-Agent Virtual Conference Playbook.md:362:[Omitted long matching line]\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:17:1. **Two separate brains with separate LLM weights, sharing one memory.** The taste brain (asks sharp questions; routes; selects context) and the reasoning brain (synthesizes answers) are different cognitive tasks. They get separate LoRA adapters on potentially different base models, fine-tuned on their own gold signals. They share Hippocampus + ontology + skills (one world-model), but their LLM weights are independent. This is the principle the rest of v3 is built on (§3.1).\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:19:[Omitted long matching line]\nARCHITECTURE-Reasoning-Agent-v3.md:21:[Omitted long matching line]\n\n[Showing results with pagination = limit: 50]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01R7onNqhV8BYzhXq32rMA1B","session_id":"75eda862-34e1-4a42-a5f6-73eb4ef1cd9a","uuid":"67ba42f6-23f3-4357-9e93-967f711fec9f","timestamp":"2026-06-23T18:18:27.749Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Ground Gandalf diagnosis of Agentic-Home"}
